WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.257
Transportation distributes benefits and burdens across people and places.

2
00:00:04.257 --> 00:00:10.089
The source chapter treats equity as a central planning responsibility rather than a final checklist.

3
00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:16.096
Benefits include access to employment, education, health care, food, recreation, and social connection.

4
00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:22.978
Burdens include crashes, pollution, noise, displacement, household cost, unreliable service, and time spent traveling.

5
00:00:22.978 --> 00:00:26.302
Equal treatment and equitable outcomes are not identical.

6
00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:31.375
Giving every neighborhood the same service level may preserve an existing disadvantage.

7
00:00:31.375 --> 00:00:35.166
Distributional equity asks who receives benefits and bears harms.

8
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:42.048
Procedural equity asks who can participate, which knowledge is accepted, and whether engagement can affect the result.

9
00:00:42.048 --> 00:00:49.279
Recognition asks whether an agency understands the histories and needs of communities that standard categories can overlook.

10
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:54.936
Civil-rights and environmental-justice frameworks shape agency duties, but compliance is a floor.

11
00:00:54.936 --> 00:01:02.867
Analysis should disaggregate results where data allows, avoid treating a large area as homogeneous, and examine people who do not drive.

12
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:07.941
Accessibility is especially useful because it connects transportation to opportunities.

13
00:01:07.941 --> 00:01:12.548
An average regional improvement can coexist with a loss for a particular group.

14
00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:15.639
Equity analysis also requires candor about tradeoffs.

15
00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:21.646
A fare change, service redesign, road project, or new technology can help some users and burden others.

16
00:01:21.646 --> 00:01:28.411
Mitigation should respond to the identified mechanism, and monitoring should test whether expected benefits arrived.

17
00:01:28.411 --> 00:01:29.741
Torrance application.

18
00:01:29.741 --> 00:01:33.287
This local example is separate from the adapted chapter.

19
00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:37.720
It cites the Torrance General Plan and Torrance Transit Tomorrow Plan.

20
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:48.360
The transit plan examined service design, operations, ridership, demographics, travel patterns, planned development, and input from riders, operators, and stakeholders.

21
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:55.769
That record offers categories for an equity review, but this episode does not reproduce its tables or make a finding.

22
00:01:55.769 --> 00:02:05.522
For a hypothetical route redesign, a Torrance reviewer could compare access to jobs, schools, medical services, and transfers before and after the change.

23
00:02:05.522 --> 00:02:14.641
The analysis should examine time of day, walking distance, frequency, reliability, fare, disability access, and the cost of a missed connection.

24
00:02:14.641 --> 00:02:21.860
Public engagement should reach riders in the places and languages they use, not depend only on an evening hearing.

25
00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.470
The reviewer should publish both improvements and losses.

26
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:34.083
If a change increases total ridership but removes the only usable trip for a smaller group, that effect should be visible and addressed.

27
00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:40.542
This is a method for asking better questions, not an official judgment about Torrance Transit service.

28
00:02:40.542 --> 00:02:50.992
